---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CalculatorCard from "../components/CalculatorCard.astro";
import FAQSection from "../components/FAQSection.astro";
import { calculators } from "../data/calculators.js";

const homeImprovementCalculators = calculators.filter(
  (calculator) => calculator.category === "Home Improvement"
);

const homeImprovementFaqs = [
  {
    question: "Should I use emergency savings for home repairs?",
    answer:
      "Yes, if the repair protects safety, prevents major damage, or keeps the home livable. Emergency savings is harder to justify for cosmetic upgrades, optional remodeling, or projects that can wait until cash is rebuilt.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are home repairs different from home improvements?",
    answer:
      "Yes. A repair fixes a problem. An improvement upgrades the home. A broken furnace, roof leak, sewer backup, or electrical hazard should be judged differently from new flooring, countertops, paint, or a larger remodel.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I finance a home repair?",
    answer:
      "Financing can make sense for urgent repairs when delaying the work would create bigger damage. It becomes risky when the monthly payment strains the budget, uses high-interest debt, or leaves no room for the next home problem.",
  },
  {
    question: "How much should I keep saved after a home repair?",
    answer:
      "There is no single number for every household, but many homeowners should try to keep at least one month of essential expenses after the repair. Older homes, unstable income, dependents, high debt, or a history of surprise repairs usually call for a larger cushion.",
  },
  {
    question: "When is a renovation worth the money?",
    answer:
      "A renovation is easier to justify when it improves daily life, fixes a long-term problem, avoids future damage, or is paid for without weakening emergency savings. It is harder to justify when it mostly creates a payment, drains savings, or is being done because of pressure, comparison, or resale optimism.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I choose the cheapest contractor?",
    answer:
      "Not automatically. The cheapest bid can be risky if it skips permits, uses weak materials, lacks insurance, or does not clearly define the work. Home projects should be judged by total risk, not just the first quoted price.",
  },
];

const relatedGuides = [
  {
    title: "Emergency Savings Calculator",
    href: "/how-much-emergency-savings-should-i-have",
    description:
      "Estimate how much emergency savings you may need before and after a major home expense.",
  },
  {
    title: "How Much Debt Is Too Much?",
    href: "/how-much-debt-is-too-much",
    description:
      "See how monthly debt pressure affects your ability to absorb repairs, renovations, and contractor financing.",
  },
  {
    title: "How Much House Can I Afford?",
    href: "/how-much-house-can-i-afford",
    description:
      "Evaluate home affordability using income, taxes, insurance, maintenance, debt, and savings.",
  },
  {
    title: "Budget Rule Calculators",
    href: "/budget-rule-calculators",
    description:
      "Compare major expenses against take-home income, savings, debt, and monthly breathing room.",
  },
  {
    title: "Medical Cost Calculators",
    href: "/medical-calculators",
    description:
      "Compare another category where urgency, savings, and debt pressure often collide.",
  },
  {
    title: "Grocery Budget Calculators",
    href: "/grocery-calculators",
    description:
      "Understand how recurring household costs affect your ability to rebuild savings after a repair.",
  },
];

const repairCategories = [
  {
    title: "Emergency Repairs",
    text:
      "Roof leaks, broken heat, electrical hazards, sewer backups, burst pipes, water intrusion, and safety problems usually deserve the fastest decision because delay can create larger costs.",
  },
  {
    title: "Preventive Maintenance",
    text:
      "HVAC service, gutter work, small plumbing fixes, appliance maintenance, caulking, drainage corrections, and minor roof work can be boring, but they may prevent more expensive failures.",
  },
  {
    title: "Livability Upgrades",
    text:
      "Projects that improve comfort, accessibility, storage, energy use, or daily function can be worthwhile when they do not damage emergency savings or add unaffordable monthly payments.",
  },
  {
    title: "Cosmetic Renovations",
    text:
      "Paint, flooring, countertops, fixtures, landscaping, and design changes can be valuable, but they should usually wait behind urgent repairs, debt control, and cash reserves.",
  },
  {
    title: "Resale-Driven Projects",
    text:
      "Projects done for resale should be judged carefully because homeowners often overestimate how much of the cost they will recover when they sell.",
  },
  {
    title: "Insurance or Claim-Related Work",
    text:
      "Storm damage, water damage, and covered losses require extra caution around deductibles, claim timing, contractor estimates, and what the insurer will actually reimburse.",
  },
];

const pressureFactors = [
  {
    title: "Urgency",
    text:
      "A repair that protects the structure, safety, heat, water, or basic use of the home receives more priority than a project that is mainly cosmetic.",
  },
  {
    title: "Cash Left After the Project",
    text:
      "The project is safer when the household still has enough money for bills, food, transportation, insurance, medical costs, and the next surprise repair.",
  },
  {
    title: "Debt Added",
    text:
      "A repair can be necessary and still create pressure if it adds a loan, credit card balance, HELOC draw, or contractor payment that strains the monthly budget.",
  },
  {
    title: "Home Age and Condition",
    text:
      "Older homes can have clustered repairs. Replacing one system may reveal problems with another, so a larger cash cushion matters.",
  },
  {
    title: "Income Stability",
    text:
      "A household with stable take-home income can recover from a repair faster than a household with seasonal, commission-based, or uncertain earnings.",
  },
  {
    title: "Ability to Delay or Phase",
    text:
      "Some projects can be split into urgent, soon, and later phases. Others must be handled immediately to avoid damage or safety risk.",
  },
];

const redFlags = [
  "The repair would use nearly all emergency savings.",
  "The project is mostly cosmetic but requires high-interest debt.",
  "The contractor is pressuring you to sign immediately.",
  "You have only one estimate for a large project.",
  "The scope is vague, verbal, or missing materials and labor details.",
  "The monthly payment would make normal bills tight.",
  "You are counting on resale value to justify a project you cannot comfortably afford.",
  "You are postponing a safety or water-damage issue to fund an optional upgrade.",
];

const reduceCostTips = [
  {
    title: "Separate must-do work from nice-to-have work",
    text:
      "Ask what solves the actual problem now and what can wait. Many projects become more affordable when the urgent repair is separated from the full dream remodel.",
  },
  {
    title: "Get more than one estimate",
    text:
      "For major repairs, multiple estimates help expose inflated pricing, unclear scope, unnecessary add-ons, or a contractor who missed part of the problem.",
  },
  {
    title: "Ask for good-better-best options",
    text:
      "A contractor may be able to quote a basic repair, a more durable repair, and a larger replacement. That gives you a financial decision instead of one scary number.",
  },
  {
    title: "Use cash for optional upgrades",
    text:
      "If the project is cosmetic or preference-based, waiting until cash is available usually creates less pressure than financing it.",
  },
  {
    title: "Phase the project",
    text:
      "Handle safety, water, heat, electrical, and structural issues first. Then schedule cosmetic or convenience upgrades after savings recover.",
  },
  {
    title: "Protect the emergency fund after the work",
    text:
      "The repair decision is not only about affording the invoice. It is also about whether you can handle normal life after paying it.",
  },
];

const assumptions = [
  "Monthly income means take-home pay available to support the household, not gross income.",
  "A necessary repair can be the right decision even when the pressure score is high.",
  "Cosmetic projects should usually face a stricter affordability test than urgent repairs.",
  "Insurance reimbursement, warranty coverage, family help, or outside assistance should reduce the true uncovered cost.",
  "Financing is treated as pressure when it creates a new monthly obligation or high-interest balance.",
  "The safest home project leaves both a completed repair and a household that can recover afterward.",
];

const internalLinks = [
  { href: "/how-much-emergency-savings-should-i-have", label: "emergency savings" },
  { href: "/how-much-debt-is-too-much", label: "debt pressure" },
  { href: "/how-much-house-can-i-afford", label: "home affordability" },
  { href: "/budget-rule-calculators", label: "budget rule calculators" },
  { href: "/grocery-calculators", label: "grocery calculators" },
  { href: "/medical-calculators", label: "medical calculators" },
  { href: "/wedding-calculators", label: "wedding calculators" },
  { href: "/calculators", label: "all calculators" },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Home Improvement Calculators | Repairs, Renovations and Savings"
  description="Use home improvement calculators to evaluate repairs, renovations, emergency savings, contractor costs, debt pressure, and household affordability."
>
  <style>
    .container {
      width: 92%;
      max-width: 1120px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 72px 0;
    }

    .hero {
      max-width: 920px;
      margin-bottom: 56px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: var(--primary);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 2px;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 6vw, 5rem);
      line-height: 1;
      margin-bottom: 24px;
      color: var(--primary);
    }

    .hero p,
    .section p,
    .verdict-bar p,
    .callout p {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      line-height: 1.85;
      color: var(--muted);
    }

    .hero p {
      max-width: 860px;
    }

    .grid,
    .info-grid,
    .link-grid,
    .split-grid {
      display: grid;
      gap: 24px;
    }

    .grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
      margin-top: 36px;
    }

    .info-grid,
    .link-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
      margin-top: 28px;
    }

    .split-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
      margin-top: 28px;
    }

    .section {
      margin-top: 76px;
      max-width: 920px;
    }

    .wide-section {
      margin-top: 76px;
    }

    .section h2,
    .wide-section h2,
    .verdict-bar h2,
    .callout h2 {
      font-size: 2rem;
      margin-bottom: 22px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .section h3 {
      font-size: 1.35rem;
      margin: 28px 0 12px;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .verdict-bar,
    .callout {
      margin-top: 56px;
      background: #f8fafc;
      border: 1px solid #dbe4ee;
      border-radius: 24px;
      padding: 32px;
    }

    .verdict-label {
      color: var(--primary);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 1.5px;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 14px;
    }

    .info-card,
    .link-card {
      display: block;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      background: white;
      border-radius: 20px;
      padding: 22px;
      text-decoration: none;
      color: inherit;
    }

    .link-card {
      transition: transform 0.15s ease;
    }

    .link-card:hover {
      transform: translateY(-2px);
    }

    .info-card h3,
    .link-card strong {
      display: block;
      margin: 0 0 10px;
      color: var(--text);
      line-height: 1.3;
      font-size: 1.08rem;
    }

    .info-card p,
    .link-card span {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.7;
      font-size: 0.96rem;
      margin: 0;
    }

    .ordered-list,
    .check-list,
    .red-flag-list {
      margin: 22px 0 0;
      padding-left: 22px;
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.8;
    }

    .ordered-list li,
    .check-list li,
    .red-flag-list li {
      margin-bottom: 12px;
    }

    .red-flag-list li::marker {
      color: #b91c1c;
    }

    .inline-links {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 12px;
      margin-top: 26px;
    }

    .inline-links a {
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 999px;
      padding: 10px 14px;
      color: var(--primary);
      text-decoration: none;
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      background: white;
    }

    .note {
      margin-top: 24px;
      padding: 18px 20px;
      border-left: 4px solid var(--primary);
      background: #f8fafc;
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.75;
      border-radius: 14px;
    }

    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      .container {
        padding: 56px 0;
      }

      h1 {
        font-size: 2.85rem;
      }

      .hero p,
      .section p,
      .verdict-bar p,
      .callout p {
        font-size: 1rem;
      }

      .section h2,
      .wide-section h2,
      .verdict-bar h2,
      .callout h2 {
        font-size: 1.65rem;
      }
    }
  </style>

  <main class="container">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="eyebrow">Home Improvement Calculators</div>

      <h1>Home Improvement Calculators for Repairs, Renovations and Household Pressure</h1>

      <p>
        Home projects are not all the same. A leaking roof, broken furnace, sewer
        backup, electrical hazard, or failing water heater is a different kind of
        decision than new flooring, a kitchen refresh, landscaping, or a cosmetic
        remodel. These calculators help you judge home repair and renovation costs
        by looking at urgency, emergency savings, debt pressure, take-home income,
        home age, and how much financial breathing room remains after the work is
        done.
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal is not to make every home expense look affordable or unaffordable.
        The goal is to separate necessary repairs from optional upgrades and show
        when a project protects the household versus when it creates a new financial
        problem.
      </p>

      <div class="inline-links">
        {internalLinks.map((link) => <a href={link.href}>{link.label}</a>)}
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="grid">
      {homeImprovementCalculators.map((calculator) => (
        <CalculatorCard
          category={calculator.category}
          title={calculator.title}
          description={calculator.description}
          href={calculator.href}
        />
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="verdict-bar">
      <div class="verdict-label">Home Repair Pressure</div>

      <h2>
        A home project becomes risky when it solves a house problem by creating a
        household money problem.
      </h2>

      <p>
        Some repairs are worth doing even when the cost hurts. If the project
        prevents water damage, protects heat, fixes unsafe electrical work, keeps
        plumbing usable, or preserves the structure, delaying can be more expensive
        than acting. But the right repair still needs a recovery plan. After the
        invoice is paid, the household still needs food, transportation, insurance,
        medical room, debt control, and enough savings to survive the next surprise.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>What Home Improvement Affordability Really Means</h2>

      <p>
        Home improvement affordability is not just the question, “Can I pay the
        contractor?” A better question is, “After this project, is the household
        still stable?” That means looking at the true uncovered cost, the savings
        left behind, the monthly payment if financed, and the urgency of the repair.
      </p>

      <p>
        A necessary repair can score as financially stressful and still be the best
        available decision. A broken furnace in winter, an active roof leak, a
        sewer problem, or a safety issue may need action even if the timing is bad.
        In those cases, the calculator should help you understand pressure, not
        shame you for spending money on the house.
      </p>

      <p>
        Optional upgrades deserve a stricter test. A cosmetic renovation should not
        usually drain emergency savings, create high-interest debt, or make the
        monthly budget fragile. If the project improves comfort but the home is
        already livable, waiting can be the financially stronger move.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        Monthly income on ShouldISpend means take-home pay available to support
        the household, not gross income before taxes and deductions.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wide-section">
      <h2>Key Cost Categories</h2>

      <p>
        Home expenses usually fall into different pressure categories. The same
        dollar amount can be reasonable or dangerous depending on what the money
        is fixing.
      </p>

      <div class="info-grid">
        {repairCategories.map((category) => (
          <div class="info-card">
            <h3>{category.title}</h3>
            <p>{category.text}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wide-section">
      <h2>How These Home Improvement Calculators Think</h2>

      <p>
        A good home repair calculator should not treat every project like a luxury
        purchase. It should weigh the project against the household’s financial
        position and the risk of waiting.
      </p>

      <div class="info-grid">
        {pressureFactors.map((factor) => (
          <div class="info-card">
            <h3>{factor.title}</h3>
            <p>{factor.text}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Methodology: How We Judge Home Repair Pressure</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend home improvement calculators look at the relationship between
        cost, urgency, cash reserves, borrowing, and recovery room. The same repair
        may be low pressure for a household with strong savings and stable income,
        but high pressure for a household that would be left with no cushion.
      </p>

      <ol class="ordered-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Start with the true uncovered cost.</strong> Insurance,
          warranty coverage, family help, grants, rebates, or seller credits should
          reduce the amount the household actually has to cover.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Separate emergency repairs from optional upgrades.</strong>
          Safety, heat, water, electrical, plumbing, structural, and active damage
          issues receive different treatment than cosmetic or preference-based
          projects.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Measure savings after the project.</strong> A project that leaves
          meaningful emergency savings is much safer than one that leaves the
          household exposed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Account for new monthly payments.</strong> Financing can be
          practical, but the payment has to fit inside the real monthly budget.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consider the home’s future repair risk.</strong> Older homes and
          homes with multiple aging systems need more cushion because one repair
          often leads to another.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Look for a recovery path.</strong> The best decisions include a
          realistic plan to rebuild savings after the repair.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="wide-section">
      <h2>Reduce Cost Tips</h2>

      <p>
        The cheapest project is not always the safest project, but most homeowners
        can reduce pressure by narrowing the scope, getting better information, and
        protecting cash.
      </p>

      <div class="info-grid">
        {reduceCostTips.map((tip) => (
          <div class="info-card">
            <h3>{tip.title}</h3>
            <p>{tip.text}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Financial Red Flags</h2>

      <p>
        These warning signs do not always mean the answer is no. They mean the
        project deserves more caution before you sign, borrow, or empty savings.
      </p>

      <ul class="red-flag-list">
        {redFlags.map((flag) => <li>{flag}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>When Spending on the House Makes Sense</h2>

      <p>
        Spending on a home can be the right move when the project protects safety,
        prevents a bigger repair, keeps the home usable, or solves a recurring
        problem. A $2,500 repair that stops water intrusion may be more financially
        responsible than a $900 cosmetic upgrade that adds no protection.
      </p>

      <p>
        It can also make sense to spend on comfort and livability when the basics
        are already stable. If emergency savings remain healthy, high-interest debt
        is not growing, and the project improves daily life, then a home upgrade
        does not have to be treated as reckless.
      </p>

      <p>
        The danger is using homeownership as a reason to approve every project.
        Owning a house creates real maintenance obligations, but it also creates
        endless opportunities to spend. The best decision is the one that keeps the
        home functioning and keeps the household financially intact.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Calculator Assumptions</h2>

      <p>
        These assumptions help keep home repair results realistic and consistent
        across ShouldISpend calculators.
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        {assumptions.map((assumption) => <li>{assumption}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="wide-section">
      <h2>Related Calculators and Guides</h2>

      <p>
        Home projects often connect to broader household affordability. A repair
        decision may depend on savings, debt, housing costs, groceries, medical
        expenses, or other major spending categories.
      </p>

      <div class="link-grid">
        {relatedGuides.map((guide) => (
          <a class="link-card" href={guide.href}>
            <strong>{guide.title}</strong>
            <span>{guide.description}</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="callout">
      <h2>Use the calculators as a pressure test, not a permission slip.</h2>

      <p>
        A low-pressure result does not mean every upgrade is necessary. A
        high-pressure result does not mean every repair should be avoided. The
        result should help you see the tradeoff clearly: what the project fixes,
        what it costs, what it does to savings, and how much flexibility remains
        afterward.
      </p>
    </div>

    <FAQSection title="Home Improvement FAQ" faqs={homeImprovementFaqs} />
  </main>
</Layout>